      'Laozi, “Old Master”, is the name attached to the Tao Te Ching (Daodejing), a book of 81 very short chapters. The oldest biography is in the Records of the Grand Historian by Sima Qian, written around 100 BC, centuries after the events it describes. It says Laozi’s family name was Li and his personal name Er, that he came from Chu in the south, and that he served as a keeper of archives at the royal court of Zhou. In one famous episode Confucius visits him to ask about ritual, and afterwards compares him to a dragon, a creature he cannot grasp. Sima Qian adds that some people said Laozi lived more than a hundred and sixty years, a sign of how much legend had already gathered around him.',
