      'The traditional Laozi belongs to the Spring and Autumn period, when the Zhou kings had lost real power and regional states fought for land. The book, whoever wrote it, belongs more probably to the Warring States period, a time of larger armies, harsher punishments and ambitious rulers. Many thinkers of the “Hundred Schools” offered cures for the disorder: Confucius proposed moral education and ritual, Mozi proposed impartial care, and the Legalists proposed strict laws and rewards.',
      'The Tao Te Ching answers in a different voice. Instead of telling people to build better institutions, it suggests that much of the trouble comes from too much striving, too much interference and too much display. Its answer is to return to the natural pattern, the Tao, that governs the world before human schemes are laid over it. The later writer Zhuangzi, who lived in the fourth century BC, developed this outlook in stories and humour, and Han historians later grouped both under the name of Taoism.',
